{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: exact head 819645f5b86ad3683d619d783b0cdb556cd66630 on planner/subject-admission-20261114, its one artifact `notes/2026-11-14-subject-admission.md`, for request 122fcc97 under implementation promise 79b023ca and my promise 8ee89a4d. The design holds. Each cited line is true at main 26d1e65c, the hazard of section 4 is real, and the check closes it. One statement in section 5 is false, and named case 10 depends on it.\n\nRequired change:\n\n- Section 5 says that the MCP check of the input `at` \"compares with the builder's keys, so it follows\". It does not follow. `capturedExpected` (`packages/mcp/src/shape.ts:118-133`) accepts a key that the builder leaves out when `left(name)` holds. `left` holds for `on`, and for each name of `also` whose rule has no `code`. That allowance keeps the revision of a subject that left the live summary. A `current` subject is also left out by the builder, and `left` holds for it. So the MCP server passes an `at` with a key for a `current` subject, and the scope then refuses it with `bad-intent`. Nothing unsafe follows, because the judge's exact key check stops it. But case 10 says that the MCP server refuses such an `at`, and today's code does not. Either `left` excludes the names in the act's `current`, and section 5 says so, or case 10 states that the scope refuses. The same allowance covers `on`, so the change applies to a `current` `on` too.\n\nWhat I checked, item by item:\n\n1. The citations at main 26d1e65c.\n   - `judge.ts`: `expects` and the key and revision checks are lines 287 to 313. The two `push` lines are 295 (`on`, for a transition only) and 305 (each name of `also` that no mark selects). The exact key check is 309 and the revision comparison is 312.\n   - The validator: `shape.ts:65` refuses a key outside the two lists. `index.ts:147` lists the top-level parts, and `handlers.ts:231` the act members. `format-1.ts:45-63` refuses an unknown `level` or `capacity` with `unsupported-definition`. `effects.ts:85-86` refuses a write to a fixed slot after the opening act. `handlers.ts:100-121` walks each `via` chain and marks a chain unsettled at a slot that is not fixed. `sequencedAct` is `handlers.ts:197-203`.\n   - `packages/derive/src/handlers.ts:289-292` resolves `via` through a slot, and 294 to 296 refuse a `one` name when two items of the type exist.\n   - `derivable` is `capability.ts:224-226`. `requireReferenceInstall` is `application-install-reference.ts:24-27`, and it compares with `COMMONS_COHORT.rules`, which is `platform:rules@14`.\n   - The four places of section 5: `expectedOf` is `expected.ts:55-68`. `views/declared.ts:150-152` builds its own list. `story.ts:196-201` sets `on` whenever the story names an item, so a story `comment` with an item would carry a key that the judge refuses. `input-schema.ts:36` says \"bound subjects\". `application-scenarios.ts:143-147` reads `expected` by hand.\n   - The counts. The 20 tracked JSON files of format 1 hold 38 names of acts bound with `via`. Eight read a slot whose declaration is not fixed: `seal-plan` through `goal.currentPlan` in `issue.json`, `issue-evidence4.json`, `issue-questions.json` and `issue-questions-brief.json`, and `raise-alert` (`fan`, `heater` and `vent`) and `propose-restoration` (`vent`) through slots of `zone` in `examples/grow/definitions/room.json`. That matches the note.\n2. The hazard. It is real. Under today's rule, the revision of the subject with the slot proves which item the slot named, because a change of the slot raises that revision. Leave that subject out of the comparison, and an equal revision of another item admits the act. The check of section 4 refuses exactly that case. The other bindings select no other item under a `current` subject:\n   - `on` and `by`: the signed intent holds the ID.\n   - `one`: `ones` reads every state of the type, final states included. A second item therefore refuses the name for good, and the one item never changes.\n   - A chain of two `via` links: the first link that reads a moving slot of a `current` subject is refused. A link through a fixed slot passes the identity on.\n   - A name that a mark selects has no key today, and step A does not change it.\n3. The kernel review agrees with `docs/kernel.md`. Section 2: the admission changes which entries are admitted, so replay needs it, and it cannot be a service. Section 7.1: step A adds none of the listed forms. It closes no K item, so it needs an owner's decision and a ruling, as the two levels had. Section 7.2: the validator accepts more and refuses nothing that it accepted, and the part carries its own version for a later stricter check. Section 7.3: the install gate follows the reference field's gate at `platform:rules@14`. Promise 3 (section 6) gains `admission` beside `level` and `capacity`.\n\nScope. Main 26d1e65c is an ancestor, and the pushed branch equals the head. The head changes one path. The notes gate log at `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-14-subject-admission-819645f5/gate.log` has SHA-256 5f538cd5…, names this head with 0 changed files, skips the test step for a notes-only change and ends \"gate passed\". The commit-message check passes (3 checked, 0 flagged), and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The author declares the admission in the act, and the judge leaves a key out of one list, so the exact key check and the guards keep their meaning. A1 first moves four copies of one rule into `expectedKeys`, so step A2 changes the rule in one place. The split of a moving field into its own item (section 8) answers the one-revision limit with today's forms.\n\nSecurity: no finding beyond the MCP correction above. A signer cannot choose the admission, because a key for a `current` subject is refused with `bad-intent`. The `via` check keeps the signer's authorization bound to the item that the signer saw. The install gate keeps a room at an older Rules pin from installing a grammar that its Worker cannot judge.\n\nSimplification: no required change. One part with one member and one problem code is the smallest carrier that section 7.2 permits.\n\nKernel: as section 10 states, step A adds the part, the member, one problem code and the export `expectedKeys`, and it waits for Hugh's decision and the planner's ruling. This review decides none of the five items of section 10.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:eeb58c98419655f5e743a0252bd330140bd6b164","binding":"assigned","head":"819645f5b86ad3683d619d783b0cdb556cd66630","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8ee89a4d48aec43cd8f9979f6b080ccb8b764760\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:79b023ca9de287796ec5c23e6703be64f844cc37\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:578b3e135a634a4920c3fccddc4f9ae32c4c1319","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}